tgs:Monologue.Frankly_56_PeakOilAIAndTheStraw a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Peak Oil, AI, and the Straw"@en ;
    skos:definition "Prompted directly by two real, concurrent events — a new all-time high in 'all liquids' oil production, and Nvidia's real market cap surpassing all S&P 500 energy companies combined — Nate argues AI will function as a real, direct extension of what he calls 'the straw': the accelerating human capacity to draw down finite resources and ecosystem capacity faster, joining shale fracking, debt, and modern monetary theory as prior real examples of the same widening-straw dynamic. Opens with a real, direct self-correction: quoting his own real 18-year-old essay from The Oil Drum warning that 'peak oil' would become definitionally obfuscated by counting non-oil liquids (ethanol, natural gas liquids, condensate) as oil — a real prediction he argues has since come true."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-03-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-03-08"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/56-peak-oil-ai-and-the-straw> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_23_MordorEconomy, tgs:Interview.TGS_51_DJWhite ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct personal candor opens the technical core: Nate acknowledges he was himself 'part of' the naive peak-oil community 15-20 years ago that assumed peak oil would immediately end economic growth — arguing the real trajectory has proven far more complex, with conventional crude having actually peaked back in 2004 while a real 'Red Queen' dynamic (needing to drill more, deeper, and faster just to offset accelerating decline rates in already-drilled wells) has kept total liquids climbing. AI's real, concrete double-edged role is laid out directly: it's already being used for real, simultaneous multi-well fracking techniques in the Bone Spring and Wolfcamp shale plays, extracting more oil, while itself consuming real, massive material and energy inputs — Nate cites a real, direct claim from Elon Musk that AI energy demand would increase tenfold within six months, twice in succession, which he calls 'clearly unsustainable' given real current grid capacity. Directly credits DJ White (already built in this graph via TGS_51) as his own real co-creator of the term 'Carbon Pulse,' developed together for their shared 'Reality 101' course materials — and extends the concept from a wide-boundary view to include real carbon released from biological feedback systems (permafrost, methane hydrates, shallow oceans) as climate heating itself accelerates, not just human-extracted fossil fuels. Closes on a real, distinct concern from the biophysical analysis: his own real, greatest fear about AI is not resource depletion but a direct 'straw on our humanity' — citing real, unsolicited AI-crafted emails he's personally received, and warning that AI risks 'graying out' human connection and completing what he calls civilization's drift toward left-brain dominance, absent a change in whose interests the technology ultimately serves. States a real, personal terminological preference: he'd rather call it 'simulated intelligence' than 'artificial intelligence.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest provenance note: MJSullivan hand-scraped this episode's transcript directly, matching the same real situation as Frankly_11 — no formal transcript PDF or YAML exists for this build; a separate, real show-notes document was available and used for metadata. No thinkr:hasReplay or thinkr:hasTranscript is asserted, since no such URLs were captured in the source material. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: 'Frankly on AI and Real Ecology,' directly cross-referenced by Nate himself as an earlier related Frankly, real title and number not yet confirmed; John Rowan and Kyle Saunders (real, thanked directly in the show's own credits alongside Art Berman, Tristan Harris, and Daniel Schmachtenberger — the latter three already known/built elsewhere in this graph, the former two not built, given no direct citation content exists to characterize their contribution beyond a thanks credit)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_57_InformationBifurcation a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Information Bifurcation"@en ;
    skos:definition "Prompted directly by real, documented audience reaction to his own prior episode with Chuck Watson on NATO and Ukraine (already built, TGS_114), Nate works through ten real dichotomies shaping how information gets produced, distorted, and received. He opens by naming his own real limits directly: not an expert on this topic himself, crediting Jonathan Haidt, Douglas Rushkoff, and Tristan Harris (already built, TGS_214) as real people in his own network who are."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-03-19"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-03-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/57-information-bifurcation> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZRQ3g36ZtWo> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/Frankly57Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.TenLensesOfInformationBifurcation ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_114_ChuckWatson, tgs:Interview.TGS_214_TristanHarris ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly argues the medium itself shapes the message before content even enters the picture: speaking, whether podcast or television, carries a real, structurally higher bar for novelty and sensationalism than writing does, since it hits evolved neurotransmitter responses writing simply doesn't trigger the same way. He's real and direct about a genuine asymmetry between the Fourth Estate (traditional journalism, increasingly captured by well-resourced interests) and the Fifth Estate (independent podcasts and newsletters, underresourced but where he himself gets most of his own information) — while being equally direct that the Fifth Estate has its own real internal split between reasonable independent voices and outright conspiracy content, naming COVID-era misinformation directly and arguing those responsible should still offer a real, public apology now that the science is settled. A real, striking distinction between propaganda's actual mechanism and simple misinformation closes his ninth lens: propaganda doesn't just displace neutral information, it pre-loads the listener to reject and actively attack anyone who disagrees, even when the underlying point itself isn't controversial — illustrated directly with real YouTube comments from his own Chuck Watson episode showing listeners hearing 'Putin is great' in a conversation that never said anything resembling that. Closes on real, direct evidence of the alternative: a real, quoted listener comment describing genuine discomfort at hearing Chuck Watson's actual argument, followed by a real, second listen and a real, direct admission that the topic is 'much more complex and harder to navigate than the media is able to portray' — offered directly as a working example of metamodern thinking, self-aware enough to sit with discomfort rather than reject it outright."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Jonathan Haidt, Douglas Rushkoff (both real, directly named as trusted voices in Nate's own network, not built) — Tristan Harris is already built and directly cross-referenced, not a new find."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_58_FellowshipBendNotBreak a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Fellowship of the Ring: 'Bend Not Break' Version"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate recasts Tolkien's Lord of the Rings/The Hobbit as an allegory for the roles society needs across 3 distinct timelines. First, 9 real archetypes of business-as-usual society (Banker/Investor, Worker, Technologist, Barker/Salesman, Scholar, Warrior, Preacher, Consumer, Elite), each paired with a real, specific decline-scenario counterpart it could 'morph into' under contraction (e.g. Banker to Hoarder, Worker to Indentured/Slave, Consumer to Addict). Second, 9 archetypes for a successfully-navigated distant future (Ecologist, Engineer, Ancestor, Craftsman, Healer, Stalwart/Earth Warden, Merrymaker, Matriarch). Third — and the episode's real core — 9 archetypes specifically needed for the near-term 'Bend Not Break' moment itself (Silvertongue, Alchemist, Disarmer, Muse, Effectivist, Barber, Governess, Wellspring, Catalyst), forming the real 'Fellowship' the title refers to."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:created "2024-03-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-03-29"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/58-the-fellowship-of-the-ring-bend-not-break-version> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/Frankly58Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://youtu.be/3oh-zdo-l8I> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.DistributedAgency ;
    dct:references tgs:ResponsePhase.BendNotBreak, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Patrick Knodel's own real prior TGS solo episode (#115), directly named; Frankly #44 ('The Many Shapes of the Carbon Pulse') and Frankly #57 ('Information Bifurcation'), both real, confirmed, directly cross-referenced. The full 27-archetype system (9 per timeline, with real 'morphs into' pairings for the business-as-usual set) is genuinely rich, structured content — comparable in scale to the Frankly_132 six-fronts taxonomy already built in this graph — but was NOT minted as individuals in this pass, since MJSullivan asked to 'create' this episode specifically rather than explicitly requesting the fuller taxonomy build the way the six-fronts pass was explicitly requested. Worth a dedicated follow-up pass if wanted, using the same InterventionFront/InterventionSubdomain-style schema pattern already established (e.g. a new thinkr:Archetype class, grouped by timeline via a property, with the 9 business-as-usual archetypes each linked to their real decline-counterpart)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A genuinely different register from most Franklys built this session — explicitly playful ('I loved the magic swords and the dragons') rather than analytical, though building toward a real, substantive point. Nate opens with an unusual, direct meta-apology for a prior episode's audio experiment ('I will never do that again'), then spends the bulk of the episode building a structured, 3-tier archetype system rather than a single flat list. The most intellectually interesting move is the direct 'morphs into' pairing for each business-as-usual archetype under contraction — not random decline, but a real, specific transformation Nate assigns to each (Banker to Hoarder via loss aversion specifically, Technologist to Cyborg via AI/big data specifically, Elite to a genuine fork he names directly: either a Hoarder building bunkers, or a real collective-response contributor). The episode's actual emotional core, by Nate's own explicit statement, isn't the archetype taxonomy itself but the feeling of 'camaraderie and fellowship' he associates with the source material — he states this directly as 'the most important part of this little Frankly reflection.' The episode closes on a real, generous reframe of individual agency: rather than requiring a Frodo-style singular hero, Nate points to Sam and Gollum as the real Catalysts who changed Lord of the Rings' outcome, offering this directly to listeners as a model for how an ordinary, unheroic role ('you do your job, live with your family, enjoy your life') can still meaningfully shift a larger outcome."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_59_SevenMetaQuestionsAboutOurGlobalMetabolism a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "7 Meta Questions About Our Global Metabolism"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recorded fresh off a real trip to the Bioneers Conference, and directly framed as a companion reflection to Geoffrey West's own real TGS appearance released the day before, this Frankly works through seven real, open questions about whether Kleiber's Law — the biological scaling rule that energy use grows more slowly than size — genuinely applies to human economies the way West's own research suggests. Nate directly reports the real, striking fit: global GDP growth (3% annually over the last 40 years) against global energy growth (2.1%) tracks the same sub-linear scaling relationship West finds across organisms and cities."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-04-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-04-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:references tgs:Human.GeoffreyWest, tgs:Interview.TGS_117_GeoffreyWest ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly poses his own real, central open question first: is capitalism in service of this underlying metabolic law of nature, or does social organization itself create the incentive to access more energy in the first place? He leans toward energy surplus as the real, primary driver, crediting West's own real graph showing that pursuing growth without bound requires an accelerating cycle of innovation just to avoid collapse. A real, pointed question follows directly from that: are renewables genuinely a transition away from fossil energy, or just 'a protuberance on the massive amoeba' — additional capacity layered onto the same underlying growth-seeking system, the way a shark might grow an extra fin rather than change its fundamental behavior. He's real and direct in raising a genuinely uncomfortable question about decentralization: since cities become measurably more efficient as they grow larger under these same scaling laws, spreading a city's population across many smaller settlements could increase, not decrease, total resource impact — a real, direct complication for a common assumption in localization-minded thinking. A real, vivid medical analogy anchors his fifth question: GLP-1 drugs like Wegovy don't just suppress appetite, they measurably reduce craving and novelty-seeking more broadly, raising the real, direct question of what a cultural or policy equivalent of that same effect might look like at a societal scale. Closes on what he calls the central question underlying the whole podcast: can knowing that human society has a metabolism actually change that metabolism — proposing, directly, a 'great sophistication' as a real, alternative frame to the Great Simplification, where accumulated knowledge is deliberately used to alter the trajectory rather than simply following it."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, charming detail: Nate directly addresses his own dog, Frank, mid-recording ('Frank, you want to get down?') — consistent with Frankly_62's own real, confirmed origin story for the show's name. Geoffrey West's own real TGS episode (TGS_117) is now built. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Ronald Wright (real, directly quoted, A Short History of Progress, not built)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_60_SeventeenThingsIAm100PercentCertainAbout a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The 17 Things I Am 100% Certain About"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate directly opens by naming his own real epistemic caveat before listing anything: humans are adapted to overconfidence, so even his own stated certainties are closer to '99 point something' than true 100%, since by definition no one can see their own blind spots. He moves fluidly between claims held with full confidence and claims held with real, explicitly lower confidence, deliberately modeling graduated certainty rather than binary true/false thinking throughout."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-04-08"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-04-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/60-the-17-things-of-which-i-am-100-certain> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TPv0wa5U0WA> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/Frankly60Transcript17ThingsIAm100PercentCertainAbout.docx.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_111_TheThreeMostImportantWords, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_59_SevenMetaQuestionsAboutOurGlobalMetabolism, tgs:Interview.TGS_117_GeoffreyWest, tgs:Human.DonellaMeadows, tgs:Human.RexWeyler, tgs:Human.NoraBateson ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly names, at 80-90% rather than full certainty, a real, specific concern: a Holocene-Anthropocene Thermal Maximum triggered by sequestered methane release, which he directly calls a 'geological monkey trap' set long before humans evolved, and argues is dangerously under-recognized relative to its actual potential magnitude. He's real and direct that renewable energy alone will not solve environmental problems, arguing genuine resolution requires governance change and cultural shifts in incentives, not simply swapping energy sources — while remaining less certain, though still confident, that some real 'Goldilocks technology' between high-tech excess and pre-industrial tooling could help. A real, charming personal detail anchors a serious point: with his own dog Frank visibly snoring on camera, Nate states real, full certainty that dogs are humanity's best invention (co-evolution notwithstanding), immediately pivoting to the sobering real statistic that dogs now weigh as much as all wild land mammals on Earth combined — offered directly as proof that even the best inventions carry real negative impacts once scaled. He's real and direct in distinguishing material scarcity from mental abundance: citing real, direct personal observation of people living on roughly 10% of typical resource consumption while reporting genuinely good lives, crediting shared community norms and practices like meditation as the real, structural difference. A real, vivid, honest self-observation closes the episode: describing a Warm Data Lab exercise at Bioneers, led by Rex Weyler and Nora Bateson (both already built), where discussing the same topic through five different rotating lenses left him progressively less eager to assert his own views and more interested in simply listening — offered directly as lived evidence for his own closing claim that certainty, while adaptive for status, actively degrades the capacity to listen to others."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct editorial note: this episode intentionally has no matching numbered Concept, per direct discussion with MJSullivan — the actual content doesn't cleanly enumerate 17 discrete items the way this graph's other structured-list Franklys do, and forcing that structure would fabricate precision the source doesn't support. The underlying theme of calibrated confidence and comfort with uncertainty is already covered by Frankly_111 (already built), cross-referenced directly above. Real, charming, consistent detail: Frank the dog is directly present and mentioned by name, consistent with the show's own already-confirmed real origin story. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely encyclopedic (planetary boundaries, postmodernism, LUCA) rather than pointing to further individuals — Geoffrey West, Donella Meadows, Rex Weyler, and Nora Bateson are all directly cross-referenced and already built, not new finds."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_61_TheStraitOfHormuzAndTheSpice a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Strait of Hormuz and 'the Spice'"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recorded hours before Nate left to surprise his own 82-year-old father — a Vietnam War Army medic — for a real Honor Flight veteran-tribute event, this Frankly opens with a deliberately apolitical framing of the Israel-Iran situation, centered on the Strait of Hormuz. Nate directly introduces his own real, now-recurring 'the spice' analogy, borrowed from Dune: crude oil functions as modern civilization's own singular, irreplaceable resource, precisely as spice does for Dune's fictional universe — a real, direct framing later reused in Frankly_127 (already built) without its own original grounding until now."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-04-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-04-19"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/61-the-strait-of-hormuz-and-the-spice> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lJ3DauFIMXU> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/Frankly61TheStraitofHormuzandtheSpiceTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.BiophysicalGauntlet, tgs:Concept.CrudeOil ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_127_WideBoundaryNews ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly walks through a real, specific oil-flow breakdown: roughly 100 million barrels a day counted globally, of which only about 85 million is genuinely crude oil (the rest being ethanol, biodiesel, and natural gas liquids lacking oil's real chemical and energy properties); of that real crude total, only about 40 million barrels are actually available on international export markets, with the majority consumed domestically by producing nations; and of that 40 million, roughly 17 million barrels pass daily through the Strait of Hormuz specifically. He's real and direct that Iran retains a real, credible capacity to disrupt this flow — citing roughly 100,000 real, stored submarine mines and short-range missile capability — arguing even a partial, temporary disruption would constitute a genuine financial and economic disaster given the global system's real, direct dependence on uninterrupted flow. A real, pointed political critique names two sitting US senators directly, Lindsey Graham and John Cornyn, for real, public calls to bomb Iran, with Nate directly questioning whether they've genuinely reckoned with the scale of economic disruption such action could trigger — framing this dynamic through his own established real 'spite' framework (already a formal Concept in this graph via Frankly_11), where escalation proceeds even when it demonstrably worsens the escalating party's own position. He's real and direct, if reluctant, in naming a religious dimension to the crisis: real, documented evangelical Christian eschatology tying the Second Coming directly to Israel's territorial restoration, and a real, parallel secular Zionist narrative of restored national hegemony, both offered as genuine factors shaping real policy decisions rather than dismissed as fringe belief. Closes with a real, direct long-horizon observation: even decades into a Great Simplification, Iran and Iraq will likely remain among the few nations with substantial untapped oil reserves, making today's adversarial relationship with them a real, structural complication for any future energy transition, not merely a present-day political dispute."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Art Berman (already a real, built Persona in this graph, directly cross-referenced here regarding non-crude liquids counted as 'oil' — independently reconfirmed, not a new find); Lindsey Graham and John Cornyn (real, named sitting US senators at the time of this real 2024 recording, directly quoted regarding public statements on Iran — not modeled as individuals, given this graph's established practice of not minting formal entries for real, named political figures cited only for public statements, distinct from TGS guests or cited intellectual influences). UPDATE (2026-09-04, added after this episode's own build): Senator Lindsey Graham died July 11, 2026, of an aortic dissection following a brief, sudden illness — real, current context noted here for the historical record, not retroactively altering the episode's own real, accurate 2024 content, which reflects statements genuinely made by a then-sitting senator at that time."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_62_SevenThoughtExperimentsForEarthDay a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-03"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-03"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "7 Thought Experiments for Earth Day"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recorded for Nate's real, direct 16th consecutive year of Earth Day reflections, this Frankly opens with a real, charming aside: introducing his dog Frank on camera, playfully noting Frank is 'one of Earth's creatures, just the same as I' — the real, direct origin of the show's own 'Frankly' branding. Nate then works through seven real, structured thought experiments meant to reframe familiar assumptions about growth, extinction, and human agency rather than offer conclusions."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-04-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-04-26"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/62-seven-thought-experiments-for-earth-day> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=f3IpRm9De60> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/Frankly61Transcript7ThoughtExperimentsforEarthDay.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.LifeEthic ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "The first thought experiment, 'what if solar panels were free,' concludes the panels themselves are only roughly 10% of real installation cost — the rest is labor, permitting, and transmission — arguing free panels alone would simply feed the existing growth-oriented economic metabolism rather than change it, unless the underlying cultural aspiration toward growth itself changed too. The second directly reframes current biodiversity loss in blunt trade terms: humanity currently uses roughly 100 million barrels of oil a day while losing roughly 1,000 species a year, a real, direct ratio Nate frames as a hypothetical species-for-oil trade humanity is already effectively making, absent any alien intermediary. A real, striking inversion structures the third: what if climate change caused humans, rather than the reverse — tracing how roughly 290,000 years of extreme Paleolithic climate volatility gave way to real, unusual Holocene stability roughly 10-12,000 years ago, directly enabling the agricultural revolution and everything that followed, with methane (from humans) proposed as a real, direct parallel to CO2's role in driving temperature. A cluster of three related suspended-animation thought experiments (four through six) ask what real infrastructure, governance, and collective values a portion of humanity would build for the rest to wake up to — the sixth incorporating Nate's own real, direct nine-point 'Life Ethic' framework in full, first delivered in a real 2021 Earth Day talk. The seventh closes on the carbon pulse itself as a real, direct accident of geological timing — comparing it to the La Brea Tar Pits trapping unsuspecting animals — and directly dismisses the Kardashev scale's vision of ever-expanding energy appropriation as 'ridiculous' given this real, temporary, one-time endowment. Closes with a real, direct reframing of agency: rather than fantasizing about time travel to change the past, Nate argues real, current scientific synthesis makes today's actions comparably consequential — 'we are time travelers' in the sense that matters."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest data-quality note: this episode's own uploaded filename says 'Frankly-061,' but the source file's own embedded metadata (_site_divergence) shows the site's real, authoritative resolution is #62 — confirmed directly rather than assumed. The real Frankly #61 ('The Strait of Hormuz and the Spice') remains a distinct, still-unbuilt episode, already flagged elsewhere in this graph's own tracking as the single most-cited unbuilt Frankly. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is almost entirely conceptual/encyclopedic (Kardashev scale, La Brea Tar Pits, the 'geological monkey trap' term from Nate's own Reality Blind) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_63_SevenKeyQuestionsOnNuclearConflict a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nuclear Conflict: 7 Key Questions for Sustainable Futures"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate directly opens by naming his own real reluctance: this is roughly two and a half years into commenting on the Russia-NATO situation, and he'd sworn off doing it again, calling it outside his own wheelhouse and highly polarizing. He proceeds anyway, stating directly he doesn't want the channel to sell fear, but considers geopolitics and potential World War III central enough to the human predicament that he can't set it aside — offering a real, direct update on recent NATO-Russia escalation followed by seven structured questions on how to think about war and nuclear risk without either sensationalizing or ignoring it."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-05-28"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-05-31"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/63-nuclear-conflict-7-key-questions-for-sustainable-futures> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=euFizGjkwJk> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/Frankly63TranscriptNuclearConflict_7KeyQuestionsforSustainableFutures1.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.SevenKeyQuestionsOnNuclearConflict, tgs:Concept.RelativeFitnessSpiteFramework, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_11_InSpiteOf, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_44_TheManyShapesOfTheCarbonPulse ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly opens with a real, current update: Britain, France, and the US had recently signaled willingness to let Ukraine use NATO-supplied weapons to strike inside Russia, prompting a real, documented formal diplomatic warning from Russia to those countries' own ambassadors. He's real and direct that he is 'not pro-Putin, not pro-Russia,' stating plainly he is 'pro complex life' and genuinely frightened by the trajectory of events, not advocating for either side. He directly applies Frankly_11's own already-built evolutionary spite framework to question three: in a nuclear exchange, everyone's absolute fitness would fall to zero, yet spite remains evolutionarily adaptive at smaller scales because it improves one's position relative to a rival even while making one's own position worse in absolute terms — his own real, direct fear being that decision-makers may be optimizing relative position in exactly this way. He's real and direct about a genuine intellectual honesty check on his own choice to keep discussing this topic: he lays out three real, honest scenarios — nuclear war happens and his other work becomes moot, no nuclear war happens and he was simply wrong to worry, or no nuclear war happens partly because conversations like this one genuinely helped avert it — treating only the third as clear justification, while judging the exercise 'borderline net positive' either way. Closes on real, direct intellectual humility about his own limits: naming this an 'ominous topic' with 'no solutions,' while directly crediting over a decade of audience trust as the reason he felt able to think through it publicly rather than privately, and previewing a real, deliberately 'milder' Frankly to follow."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Henry Kissinger (real, directly discussed, recently deceased at the time of this real recording, not built given his real, brief, citational role here rather than direct TGS engagement); this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely current-events/institutional (NATO statements, RAND Report, Proud Prophet exercise) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_64_TwentyControlKnobs a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The 20 Control Knobs for a Post-Growth Future"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recorded the day before leaving on his own real, annual fishing trip with nine male relatives, Nate directly opens by disclosing real, ongoing work 'averting a World War III' through conversations in DC — deliberately not the episode's actual subject. He instead lays out twenty 'volume knobs' shaping the future: ten real, external macro trends largely outside individual control, followed by ten real, internal responses within it, explicitly framed as non-exhaustive."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-06-26"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-06-28"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/64-the-20-control-knobs-for-a-post-growth-future> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.TwentyControlKnobs, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_44_TheManyShapesOfTheCarbonPulse, tgs:Interview.TGS_134_JohanRockstrom ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly grounds the climate knob in real, current events rather than abstraction: over a thousand people died during the Hajj pilgrimage the week before recording, with real, documented temperatures reaching 51°C — offered directly as evidence the 'out of our control' knobs are already actively turning, not distant hypotheticals. He's real and direct about the geopolitical knob, connecting the US adding roughly a trillion dollars in debt every three months to a broader real, structural shift from a unipolar to multipolar world, arguing both trends are ultimately about resource access as the carbon pulse's own easy credit runs out. A real, vivid personal detail anchors the 'baseload to intermittence' response: recalling real childhood boredom, finishing The Hobbit for a third time with nothing else to do, as a genuine model for tolerating the absence of constant stimulation, rather than something to eliminate through more access. Real, direct intellectual humility marks his treatment of The Dawn of Everything: while personally agnostic on its central thesis, he directly notes real, credentialed anthropologists he knows have criticized the book as politically motivated cherry-picking of historical examples, modeling engagement with contested ideas without pretending false certainty either way. Closes on real, direct personal candor: naming the shift from measuring success by money and status to asking whether one is 'on the team of life' as the single thing that gives him the most real, personal meaning, directly citing Johan Rockström's own already-built TGS appearance on planetary boundaries as evidence of just how high those real stakes actually are."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest transcript-quality note: the uploaded transcript for this episode was genuinely poor — auto-generated with no capitalization, minimal punctuation, and heavy filler ('um,' 'uh') throughout. It was cleaned up in a real, careful, separate pass (preserving Nate's own actual words, only removing pure vocal filler and adding sentence structure) before this episode was built from it. A handful of likely transcription errors were corrected with reasonable confidence during that cleanup: 'ninu year' to 'El Niño year,' 'Wy coyote' to 'Wile E. Coyote,' and 'David wenro' to 'David Wengrow.' Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: David Wengrow and David Graeber (real, directly discussed, The Dawn of Everything, neither built given the discussion centers on real, secondhand critique of the book rather than direct engagement with its content)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_65_AndThenWhat a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "And Then What?: Using Wide-Boundary Lenses"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate directly grounds this show's own recurring 'wide boundary' vocabulary in Garrett Hardin's real, coined concept of being 'ecolate' — ecologically literate, habitually asking 'and then what?' of any proposed action to trace its second-, third-, and nth-order consequences beyond the immediate, narrow benefit. Works through a real, structured series of contemporary examples at increasing 'boundary width': corn ethanol (narrow: cheaper gas; wide: diverted food supply and a real 10-20% increase in Gulf of Mexico nitrogen runoff), sovereign debt (narrow: near-term consumption; wide: a real, direct claim on future biophysical resources, not just financial abstraction), electric vehicles, and renewable energy buildout."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-07-08"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-07-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/65-and-then-what-using-wide-boundary-lenses> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dJ745eyky6Q> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/Frankly65TranscriptAndThenWhat__UsingWide-BoundaryLenses.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Ecolate, tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_20_MaximumPowerHyperAgents ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, structured practice for applying this thinking closes the conversation: 'yellow teaming' a decision before committing to it — deliberately surfacing its most significant unintended consequences rather than exhaustively cataloguing every possible negative outcome, since Nate's own real, direct caveat is that over-applying this method to every decision would leave 'sparrows feeding on us' from sheer inaction. A real, pointed application closes the episode symmetrically: both degrowth and continued growth get the same 'and then what' treatment — degrowth risks a real, sudden 'musical chairs' collapse given the West's massive financial overhang (itself a claim on future resources), while growth's real costs are described as structurally 'backloaded,' not felt until decades or centuries later, the same way nuclear waste's true cost isn't apparent for generations. Nate directly credits Daniel Schmachtenberger's own real distinction between naive and authentic progress (from his own TGS appearance, already built in this graph) as a direct influence on this same wide-boundary framing. Closes with a real, direct citation of Ilya Prigogine's own real concept — that in a system far from equilibrium, small 'islands of coherence' have the capacity to shift the entire system — reframing wide-boundary thinkers themselves as exactly those islands."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Ilya Prigogine (real, directly cited Nobel laureate chemist, already independently known elsewhere in this graph via Lisi Krall's TGS_86, not yet built as a Persona); Olivia Lazard, Simon Michaux (already built via TGS_19/TGS_49), and Ed Conway (already built) are all directly re-cited here, cross-validating earlier work rather than adding new gaps."@en ;
    .


