tgs:Monologue.Frankly_68_OvershootAndItsSevenFundamentalDrivers a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Overshoot and Its 7 Fundamental Drivers"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recorded the day after Nate ran a real, direct 103°F fever from what he suspects was a mild bout of sunstroke during a hot, humid bike ride, this Frankly lays out his own real, structured Seven Fundamental Drivers of Overshoot in full for the first time (now a formal Concept in this graph). Opens with a real, direct correction to the prior week's Frankly on solutions: the pool of genuinely effective solutions shrinks over time as intervention windows close, making the show's own real, stated purpose the deliberate expansion of the Overton window to keep pace with that shrinking pool, not simply cataloguing acceptable ideas."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-07-23"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-08-02"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/68-overshoot-and-its-7-fundamental-drivers> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZDN8ABCIDyg> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Frankly68TranscriptOvershootandIts7FundamentalDrivers.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.SevenFundamentalDriversOfOvershoot, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.Ecolate ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly grounds the Carbon Pulse driver in a real, striking statistic: total mammalian biomass on Earth today, humans plus livestock plus wild mammals combined, is roughly 700% what it was 10,000 years ago, offered as real, direct evidence of how thoroughly ancient stored energy has inflated the biosphere's own apparent carrying capacity. He's real and direct in naming a specific, real, contemporary example of driver four (lack of recognition): Elon Musk's real, public claim that Earth could support 80 billion humans, which Nate directly calls 'the most delusional statement I've ever heard,' arguing it would produce 'a planet full of dead locusts and smoking embers,' not a functioning biosphere. Closes with a real, direct, briefly-sketched set of seven possible antidotes, mirrored to the drivers themselves: energy-surplus decline, the biosphere's own real, direct enforcement of reduced carrying capacity through worsening droughts and floods, a genuine cultural shift from human-centered to earth-centered values, biophysically-tethered money creation, syntropic (rather than merely extractive) technology, and — closing on what he calls 'maybe firstly' — individual behavioral change starting at the level of everyday micro-decisions."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "SUPERSEDED 2026-09-04, per MJSullivan's own direct editorial decision for the WWNS series: this episode's Seven Fundamental Drivers framework predates, and is treated going forward as superseded by, Nate's own later, more structured Framework for Action (Frankly_132, already built). WWNS content should not cite this episode or tgs:Concept.SevenFundamentalDriversOfOvershoot as current framing — Frankly_132 is the real, current reference point instead. Kept here as a real, historical episode in its own right, not deleted, since it's still real and still happened; just no longer treated as Nate's own live, operative framework. Real, honest, human detail: Nate directly discloses recording this Frankly the day after a real 103°F fever, joking he can blame any mistakes on it — 'this podcast and running this NGO aren't really a job anymore, they're my life,' offered as real, direct explanation for why the topic came to him even while unwell. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Jared Diamond (real, directly cited, his book Collapse and its real dam-proximity cognitive-dissonance example, not built)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_69_GoldilocksTechnology a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Goldilocks Technology – A Preliminary Checklist"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate directly extends a concept already explored in an earlier, real TGS episode, laying out preliminary criteria for what he calls Goldilocks Technology — innovation calibrated between dopaminergic overreach and a naive retreat from problem-solving altogether. He opens by directly naming the real, current economic moment as an anomalous era already ending, arguing the kind of innovation that got humanity here is not the kind needed for what comes next."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-08-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-08-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/69-goldilocks-technology-a-preliminary-checklist> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BzC0AqN40U4> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Frankly69TranscriptGoldilocksTechnology.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.GoldilocksTechnology, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_121_WideBoundaryNews, tgs:Human.DanielSchmachtenberger, tgs:Human.RomanKrznaric ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly names a real, structural precondition for better technology: current prices don't reflect real ecological cost, meaning governance upstream of individual designers and engineers — not just individual values or consumer choice — has to correct pricing before genuinely appropriate technology can out-compete extractive alternatives on its own economic terms. He's real and direct that even celebrated green-technology companies remain real, direct beneficiaries of substantial government subsidy, complicating any simple 'the market will provide' narrative around clean innovation. A real, concrete historical counter-example anchors his optimistic case: Edo-period Japan sustained a genuinely functioning circular economy for centuries under real resource constraint, offered directly as proof durable low-throughput societies aren't merely theoretical. Real, current material specifics ground the checklist directly: sodium batteries as a real, viable alternative reducing dependence on constrained lithium supply chains, and a real, sobering figure that only about 8.5% of the global economy currently operates circularly, offered as the honest baseline any 'Goldilocks' technology has to improve on. Closes by directly crediting Daniel Schmachtenberger's own real 'naive progress' framing and Roman Krznaric's own real historical work as intellectual anchors for judging whether a given technology counts as genuine progress or just accelerated extraction with better branding."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own real companion TGS episode on Goldilocks Technology, directly cross-referenced in the show notes, remains unbuilt in this graph."@en ;
    .


