tgs:Monologue.Frankly_74_TheLamentOfTheBigfoot a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Lament of the Bigfoot"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate reads a real poem he wrote 20 years earlier this same month, at age 38, shortly after leaving Wall Street and starting his PhD — written from the real, imagined perspective of the last living Bigfoot, mourning a mate and children lost to logging and human expansion, closing on an image of real, violent retaliation against loggers threatening its home. He uses the poem directly to reflect on how his own thinking has and hasn't changed since, opening with a real, direct catalogue of the era's polarized dualities: pro-human versus anti-human, mechanistic versus animistic, natalist versus anti-natalist."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-10-07"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-10-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/74-the-lament-of-the-bigfoot> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=a9iQISZjEME> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Frankly74TranscriptLamentoftheBigfoot.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_121_PellaThiel, tgs:Human.AtossaSoltani, tgs:Human.JoannaMacy, tgs:Work.Ishmael ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly names his own real evolution since writing the poem: he still cares about wild nature exactly as much as he did at 38, but no longer believes an abrupt human collapse would actually be good for the natural world, given the real, direct risk of wars and ecological damage that would likely accompany it — a shift toward 'Bend Not Break' as his own real, considered alternative to either denial or collapse-as-solution. He directly credits his own real friend Pella Thiel (already built), an eco-psychologist, for introducing him to Joanna Macy's real 'Council of All Beings' practice — a group exercise where participants speak on behalf of a chosen animal or natural feature, which Thiel reports often leaves human participants genuinely saddened once they realize how comparatively little humans, framed this way, seem to contribute back to the whole. He's real and direct that changing values alone isn't sufficient: humanity's own real productive base runs overwhelmingly on ancient stored carbon, and reversing course requires genuine biophysical alternatives — regenerative grazing, restored wetlands, reforestation — not simply a shift in how people feel about nature. A real, direct policy sketch closes his practical argument: taxes payable only in a currency earned specifically through ecological restoration work, offered as one concrete way to make regenerative activity actually count economically rather than remain a moral aside. Closes on real, direct uncertainty about his own timeline and safety: acknowledging he doesn't know whether it will still be safe to speak this openly in five months or five years, before closing with Mary Oliver's own real poem on choosing a hidden nest of bird's eggs over the noise and cleverness of modern life."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest note: both poems discussed in this episode — Nate's own 'The Lament,' and Mary Oliver's closing poem — are described and paraphrased here rather than quoted at length, per real copyright discipline; the full text of each is available directly in the real source transcript linked above. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely encyclopedic (planetary boundaries, net primary productivity) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_75_SomebodysGonnaWin a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-10"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-10"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Somebody's Gonna Win"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recorded roughly three weeks before the 2024 US presidential election, Nate directly states his own real, explicit position as 'apolitical, nonpartisan,' arguing both major parties are structurally 'in thrall to the economic Superorganism' regardless of which wins — and offers a real, direct set of practical recommendations for how individuals and communities might respond well regardless of the outcome."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-10-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-11-01"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/75-somebodys-gonna-win> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KleRyQifkz4> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/Frankly75TranscriptSomebodysGonnaWin.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_12_TheGlobalStakesOfTheUSElection, tgs:Human.PierreTeilhardDeChardin ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly names his own real, four-part filter for evaluating people that deliberately sets aside conventional left-right categorization: whether someone is aware of the real, converging predicament, whether they genuinely care about the future, whether they use a wide- versus narrow-boundary lens, and whether they hold their own views with real, calibrated nuance versus fanatical certainty. He's real and direct that his own real, explicit framing of the 'biophysical pyramid' inverts the culturally dominant one: rather than money and politics sitting atop technology, the biosphere and its real, freely-given ecosystem services should sit at the actual foundation, with energy and non-renewable materials next, and technology, money, and politics only above that — arguing election-season discourse inverts this hierarchy precisely when stress and fracturing are highest. He's real and direct in naming one specific, factual policy distinction between the two parties — their real, differing willingness to even discuss climate change — while maintaining his own broader, explicitly structural critique that both remain subordinate to the Superorganism's own growth-optimizing incentives regardless of that difference. A real, direct personal disclosure closes his own framing: reading real, mixed YouTube comments on the Every/Gromen roundtable (already built as RR-12) the same week, some frustrated the conversation wasn't more ecological, others calling it his best content yet — offered directly as lived proof of his own point that this is 'a multi-issue podcast because it's a multi-issue world.' Closes on a real, direct, practical list of recommendations regardless of outcome: setting a personal goal unrelated to the election, stopping tribal doomscrolling, deliberately seeking out and simply listening to someone voting the opposite way without rebuttal, and organizing local, explicitly nonpartisan conversations about community resilience — closing with a real, direct quote from Pierre Teilhard de Chardin (already built) on the 'sensual longing for communion with others who have a large vision.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct closure of the final citation gap flagged from Frankly_137's own show notes, completing that episode's own real, traceable citation chain. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely current-events/institutional (BRICS Kazan summit coverage, Gallup polling on election issues, Hurricane Helene reporting) rather than pointing to further individuals — Michael Every and Luke Gromen are both already built via RR-12, not new finds."@en ;
    .


